def add(sub,block,title,why,facts,static,analysis,trap,mains,src='PRS',day=0,slug=None,ap=False,p='P1'):
 keys=['E12-'+x for x in src.split(',')] if src else [];extra={}
 if slug:
  art=next(a for a in D['2026-05']['articles'] if a['url'].endswith('/'+slug));k='V'+str(D['2026-05']['articles'].index(art)+1).zfill(2);source(k,'Vision IAS · May 2026 · '+art['title'],art['url'],'Public body retrieved and selected lead/substantive passages read; not a full article/issue or full underlying-primary review.');keys.append('E12-'+k);extra=dict(magazine_edition='2026-05',magazine_posted='2026-06-30',content_type='magazine-supplement')
 J.append(dict(id=f'E12-2605-{len(J)+1:03}',date=f'2026-05-{day:02}' if day and not slug else '2026-05-31',date_precision='day' if day and not slug else 'month',subject=sub,block=block,title=title,why=why+(' May is the editorial coverage index; the public edition was posted on 30 June. Event/release dates can differ.' if slug else ''),facts=facts.split('|'),static_link=static,analysis=analysis.split('|'),trap=trap,mains=mains,priority=p,ap=ap,sources=keys,status='Historical May-indexed snapshot; not certification of current law, latest data or completed implementation',checked='2026-11-07',evidence_access='Source registry records actual reading and access limits. Static links, analysis and exam guidance are original.',**extra))
add('Polity & Governance','01 · Courts and ordinance power','Supreme Court capacity: 37 judges plus the CJI','The 16 May Ordinance took effect at once.','Ordinance 1 of 2026 substitutes thirty-seven for thirty-three in section 2 of the 1956 Act.|The sanctioned total therefore rises from 34 to 38 when the Chief Justice is included.|Article 123 gives an ordinance statutory force, subject to constitutional legislative control and the six-week reassembly rule.','Article 124 permits Parliament to prescribe the number beyond the Chief Justice; changing the statutory ceiling is not a constitutional amendment.','More posts can permit additional benches, but vacancies, case management, registry support and litigation inflow also affect delay.|Distinguish a sanctioned post, an appointment, a judge taking oath and actual bench capacity when evaluating reform.','Do not write 37 including the CJI, assume all posts were immediately filled, or certify the Ordinance’s November status without checking replacement/lapse.','How can judicial capacity reform reduce delay without sacrificing reasoned justice?',src='PRS,SC',day=16)
add('Society & Welfare','01 · Central labour rules — working conditions','OSH rules: weekly hours and safe night work','Central Rules were notified on 8 May.','The inspected Rule 64 limits work in a covered establishment to 48 hours a week.|Rule 83 requires written consent for women’s night/early work, alongside transport and other safety conditions.|Lighting, accessible facilities, emergency contact and compliance with workplace sexual-harassment law are substantive safeguards.','Labour is a Concurrent List field; identify the appropriate government, establishment coverage and operative rule before applying a central instrument to an AP workplace.','Night-work access can widen opportunity only if workers can refuse without coercion and safety is actually funded.|Inspections and worker grievance channels complement digital records; a permission clause is not evidence of safe practice.','Do not universalise every central rule to every AP establishment or equate 48 weekly hours with an unconditional 12-hour daily norm.','Balance women’s labour-market opportunity with consent, safety and enforceability.',src='PRS,OSH',day=8)
add('Society & Welfare','02 · Central labour rules — gig eligibility','Gig benefits: registration, engagement days and scheme conditions','Selected Social Security Central Rules were independently inspected.','Registration provisions address gig/platform workers aged at least 16, using the designated portal and prescribed documents.|Benefit eligibility includes at least 90 engagement days with one aggregator or 120 cumulatively across aggregators in the previous financial year, plus scheme-specific conditions.|For the multi-aggregator calculation, working with three aggregators on one calendar day can count as three days: it is not necessarily 120 distinct calendar days.','Registration, eligibility and actual enrolment/payment under a notified scheme are separate stages.','Portability needs reliable worker-controlled records of engagement and an accessible correction process.|Thresholds can exclude intermittent workers; evaluate coverage without assuming registration automatically confers every salaried-worker benefit.','Do not substitute 120 distinct calendar days for the rule’s cumulative counting, or promise automatic EPF/ESI payments to every registrant.','How should portable social protection accommodate fragmented platform work?',src='PRS,SOC',day=8)
add('Society & Welfare','03 · Central labour rules — industrial relations','Industrial-relations rules: representation and procedure','The May review describes notified central industrial-relations machinery.','Rules address recognition of trade unions, Works Committees and Grievance Redressal Committees.|Procedures cover strike/lock-out notices and applications for covered lay-off, retrenchment or closure cases.|Model standing orders address mining, manufacturing and services; applicability depends on the parent Code and establishment category.','A procedure prescribed for permission-requiring establishments is not a new universal permission requirement for every employer.','Genuine representation and timely grievance resolution can reduce disputes before they become work stoppages.|For AP analysis, inspect central versus state jurisdiction, thresholds and actual institutions before asserting a uniform workplace rule.','Do not confuse a notice period, an application deadline, permission and an absolute prohibition on industrial action.','Evaluate industrial peace through representation, predictable procedure and effective grievance redress.',day=8)
add('Economy','01 · Central labour rules — wage setting','Floor wage versus minimum wage: different legal functions','The 8 May wage rules specify calculation and consultation processes.','The central floor-wage process considers minimum living standards and consultation with states/Board.|Variable dearness allowance is recalculated before 1 April and 1 October using CPI for Industrial Workers.|The inspected rules provide an eight-hour normal day for daily-wage employees and a 48-hour weekly ceiling for other wage periods.','The central floor is a benchmark; appropriate-government minimum wages and inflation adjustments are not the same administrative act.','Enforcement must address coverage, classification and contractor payment chains, not merely publish a headline rate.|Do not infer the general retail CPI or its rebasing automatically replaces CPI-IW in a specifically prescribed formula.','These rules do not by themselves announce one new rupee wage applicable to every worker across India.','How can wage-setting combine a credible floor, regional needs and enforceable payment?',src='PRS,WAGE',day=8)
add('Polity & Governance','02 · Rural employment — draft rules and transition','VB–G RAM G: draft allocation rules before commencement','May consultations must be separated from the Act’s stated 1 July commencement.','Drafts propose using 16th Finance Commission tax-devolution parameters for normative inter-state allocation under a centrally specified methodology.|From the second year, specified performance conditions may influence part of allocation.|Drafts provide DBT of wages/unemployment allowance, with exceptional central exemptions; comments were invited until 21 June.','A draft subordinate instrument is not a final rule, and an enacted law with future commencement is not already an operative replacement on every May date.','Need-sensitive allocation must avoid penalising low-capacity states twice through both deprivation and weak reporting.|For AP, examine transition of outstanding liabilities, local demand, social audit and bank access before declaring old entitlements extinguished in May.','Do not call the draft formula final or infer MGNREGA ceased operating on the day consultation drafts appeared.','Assess fiscal predictability and demand-responsive protection during rural-employment transition.',day=22)
add('Economy','02 · Liquidity guarantees','ECLGS 5.0: guarantee coverage is not debt forgiveness','The 5 May approval targets temporary liquidity pressures linked to West Asia disruption.','Eligible standard accounts include businesses with existing working-capital limits and scheduled passenger airlines with outstanding credit as of 31 March.|Additional business credit is up to 20% of peak utilised Q4 FY2025–26 working capital, capped at ₹100 crore; eligible airlines have a distinct ceiling up to ₹1,500 crore.|Coverage is 100% for MSMEs and 90% for non-MSMEs/airlines; business tenor is five years including one-year moratorium, versus seven/two for airlines.','NCGTC guarantees lenders against eligible default risk; borrowers still owe the loan and moratorium is not a grant.','Guarantees can prevent a liquidity shock becoming insolvency, but appraisal and monitoring remain necessary.|The sanction window runs from NCGTC guideline issue to 31 March 2027; Cabinet approval and actual loan disbursement are distinct.','Do not apply 20% to the sanctioned but unused limit, equate a guarantee with a waiver, or count targeted credit as paid expenditure.','When can emergency guarantees support viable firms without encouraging poor lending?',src='PRS,ECLGS',day=5)
add('Economy','03 · Industrial parks — implementation guidelines','BHAVYA: land readiness, SPVs and conditional assistance','May guidelines operationalise the earlier industrial-park scheme.','The programme envisages 100 parks over 2026–27 to 2031–32, with 50 projects in the first phase.|The reviewed eligibility includes at least 90% encumbrance-free land possession at application and delegated planning/development powers to the SPV.|Funding is conditional: up to ₹1 crore per acre in the specified framework; private-developer collaborations face a lower ₹50 lakh/acre or 50% infrastructure-cost limit, whichever is lower.','A scheme envelope, selected DPR, SPV equity contribution and completed industrial occupancy are different milestones.','For AP, appraise connectivity, water, serviced land, land obligations and realistic tenant demand alongside incentive size.|An encumbrance-free certificate does not remove environmental or rehabilitation responsibilities; land value also constrains contribution.','Do not call the per-acre maximum an unconditional grant for every industrial-estate acre or treat the 100-park target as completed.', 'How should industrial parks be selected to produce additional, sustainable economic activity?',day=23)
add('Economy','04 · Insurance investment — legal layers','Insurance FDI: enabling amendment, FEMA rules and LIC exception','May’s FEMA amendment changes the investment framework.','The review reports up to 100% foreign investment under the automatic route in insurance companies following the 2025 enabling Act.|LIC remains separately capped at 20% under the reviewed framework.|Residency requirements are relaxed to require at least one among chairperson, managing director and CEO to be a resident Indian citizen.','An automatic investment route removes a specified prior approval requirement; it does not remove sectoral licensing, prudential or policyholder rules.','New capital can support underwriting and distribution, but governance, claims practice and solvency determine consumer outcomes.|Track the Act, FEMA rules and applicable IRDAI directions separately rather than assuming all changed on one date.','Do not extend the 100% figure to LIC or claim foreign ownership guarantees universal low-cost insurance.',day=2,mains='Assess the benefits and safeguards needed when liberalising insurance capital.')
add('Economy','05 · Recovery conduct — consultation','Loan-recovery drafts: dignity in technology-enabled collection','RBI invited comments on 20 May draft amendments.','Drafts restrict device disabling, with a limited device-finance exception subject to overdue/noticing conditions and protected emergency functions.|They propose holding referral to recovery agents while a relevant borrower grievance is unresolved.|Proposed first-visit notice is at least one day electronically or three days by letter, with trained/certified recovery agents.','A valid debt does not eliminate privacy, safety and fair-recovery obligations; a draft safeguard is not automatically operative law.','Digital controls can become coercive if access to work, communication or emergency help is removed.|Lenders need auditable vendor oversight, accessible complaint resolution and proportional recovery rather than outsourcing accountability.','Do not describe every proposed restriction as final, or infer a borrower’s complaint automatically cancels the debt.','How should regulators curb coercive digital recovery without undermining legitimate credit discipline?',day=20)
add('Economy','06 · Mutual funds — third-party drafts','Third-party mutual-fund payments: consent and traceability','SEBI’s 20 May draft addresses specified scenarios.','Proposals cover employer salary-deduction investment facilities and eligible distributor commission paid as units.|A donation channel is discussed with appropriate investor mandates.|Proposed safeguards include KYC of payer/beneficiary and an auditable, non-cash trail through segregated accounts.','Payment by another person, legal ownership of units and a tax-deductible donation are distinct concepts.','Salary-linked investment can reduce transaction friction, but employees need genuinely voluntary mandates and easy correction.|Segregation and verification matter because convenience can create laundering or mis-selling channels.','Do not treat the consultation as unrestricted permission for any unidentified third party to fund any folio.','Evaluate convenience and investor protection in third-party investment payments.',day=20)
add('Economy','07 · CSR and social finance','Zero coupon zero principal: philanthropy, not a return-bearing bond','MCA’s 29 May release explains the 27 May amendments.','Subscription to qualifying SSE-issued ZCZP instruments becomes an eligible CSR activity.|A company’s spending on this route cannot exceed 10% of its total CSR expenditure in that financial year.|The instrument pays neither coupon nor principal redemption; qualifying NPOs bear project-execution/evaluation responsibilities.','A social stock exchange financing instrument need not deliver a financial return: assess its specified philanthropic purpose and accountability.','Simplified company compliance shifts attention to NPO reporting, credible outcomes and safeguards against related-party diversion.|The percentage base is CSR expenditure, not net profit, turnover or the company’s entire cash balance.','Do not interpret zero principal as a risk-free investment repaid at maturity or 10% of corporate profit.','How can social-finance instruments expand credible giving without diluting CSR accountability?',src='PRS,CSR',day=29)
add('Agriculture','01 · Kharif prices — season and cost basis','Kharif MSP 2026–27: distinguish price, cost and procurement','The 13 May CCEA decision covers 14 kharif crops.','Common paddy MSP is ₹2,441/quintal, up ₹72; Grade A is ₹2,461.|Sunflower has the highest absolute increase of ₹622/quintal; jowar hybrid’s percentage increase is higher because bases differ.|The published cost basis includes paid-out costs and imputed family labour, not every broader C2 opportunity-cost component.','MSP announcement is a price-policy decision, not a guarantee every farmer sells all output at that price.','AP analysis should connect paddy/groundnut choices to irrigation, procurement access, quality standards and payments.|Compare like varieties, seasons and units; ₹/quintal is not ₹/tonne and nominal growth is not automatically real income growth.','Do not equate the 1.5-times stated cost benchmark with 1.5 times every individual farmer’s full C2 cost or assured universal procurement.','Can MSP support diversification when procurement and water incentives remain uneven?',src='PRS,MSP',day=13)
add('Agriculture','02 · Sugarcane — recovery-linked price','Sugarcane FRP: ₹365 is tied to recovery','The May approval concerns the 2026–27 sugar season.','The reviewed base FRP is ₹365/quintal at 10.25% recovery.|A ₹3.56/quintal adjustment applies per 0.1 percentage-point recovery change around the base.|The review specifies no further deduction below 9.5% recovery.','Sugar recovery measures sugar obtained relative to cane; FRP differs from a state-advised price and from a general kharif MSP.','Higher announced cane prices matter only with timely mill payment and viable recovery/processing economics.|For AP, examine water use, mill liquidity and farmer arrears rather than infer profits from the headline price alone.','Do not say ₹365 applies identically at every recovery rate or confuse percentage points with a percentage change.','Assess price regulation and timely payment in sugarcane value chains.',day=5)
add('Agriculture','03 · Fibre productivity','Cotton mission: yield, quality and resilient farm incomes','The 5 May mission approval covers 2026–27 to 2030–31.','The review rounds the outlay to about ₹5,659 crore.|Focus areas include pest/disease-resistant high-yielding seeds, production technologies and quality-testing infrastructure.|Initial coverage is described as 140 districts in 14 cotton-producing states, not all districts immediately.','Production equals area multiplied by yield; quality, input costs and prices also determine farm income.','AP applications include suitable seed advice, integrated pest management, contamination control and traceable marketing.|Do not infer that a sanctioned mission proves a particular district received funds or farmers achieved the target yield.','Higher national output or a new seed variety alone does not establish lower pesticide costs, guaranteed prices or universal pest resistance.','How can cotton productivity improve without shifting excessive risk onto farmers?',day=5)
add('Society & Welfare','04 · Food-system delivery','SARTHAK-PDS: handling support and technology convergence','The 27 May approval continues the umbrella framework to March 2031.','The reviewed central outlay for the extension is ₹25,530 crore.|Support combines intra-state grain movement/handling and fair-price-shop dealer margins with SMART-PDS modernisation.|The policy operates within NFSA delivery, not a new universal cash income scheme.','Allocation, offtake, physical delivery and household receipt are distinct stages in food-security administration.','Reliable dealer incentives and logistics matter alongside authentication technology.|For AP, audit failed transactions, stock reconciliation, accessibility and offline safeguards; do not make biometric success a substitute for entitlement delivery.','Do not equate a digital record with grain physically received or assume a modernisation allocation expands every household’s legal quota.','How can PDS modernisation improve accountability without excluding eligible households?',day=27)
add('Environment','01 · Coal conversion — scheme design','Surface gasification: import substitution is not zero-carbon energy','The 13 May approval provides a ₹37,500 crore incentive envelope.','Financial support is at most 20% of eligible plant-and-machinery cost, paid in four equal milestone-linked instalments.|Caps are ₹5,000 crore/project and ₹12,000 crore/entity group; the ₹9,000 crore/product cap explicitly excepts synthetic natural gas and urea.|The scheme envisages about 75 MT gasification; the national 2030 target is 100 MT, not the same denominator.','Gasification uses controlled partial oxidation to make syngas, mainly CO and hydrogen; downstream conversion is not inherently carbon-free.','Appraisal must compare lifecycle emissions, water, ash handling, capture performance and import-substitution economics.|Unlike April’s underground gasification, this scheme concerns surface facilities; do not merge their land, subsurface and closure risks.','Do not apply the product cap without its SNG/urea exception, call projected investment realised, or describe all coal-derived hydrogen as green.','Evaluate surface gasification through energy security, fiscal additionality and environmental costs.',src='PRS,COAL',slug='scheme-for-promotion-of-surface-coallignite-gasification-projects')
add('Polity & Governance','03 · Evidence and migration policy','Demographic-change committee: remit is not a finding','The 26 May review reports a high-level committee chaired by retired Justice P. P. Navlekar.','Its remit includes studying demographic changes and underlying factors, including alleged illegal immigration.|It may recommend border-management, deportation-process and Centre–state coordination measures.|The initial reporting period is one year, with a possible six-month extension.','Population trends cannot alone establish an individual’s citizenship or immigration status; lawful identification and due process are separate requirements.','Reliable census, migration and administrative evidence must be distinguished from political inference or correlation.|Rights-respecting procedure and transparent methods are necessary when studying community-level changes.','Do not treat committee constitution as proof that any particular community or person is an unlawful immigrant.','How should demographic-policy research combine credible evidence with individual rights?',day=26)
add('Economy','08 · Transport fitness and regulatory evidence','Vehicle fitness: retesting, geo-tagged records and conflicts','The 8 May vehicle-rule amendment revises testing procedures.','The reviewed rules categorise a vehicle as end-of-life if it is not declared fit within 180 days after being declared unfit.|Specified fitness tests must be video-recorded with geo-tags and uploaded through the prescribed app.|Ownership/operator restrictions address conflicts, including local vehicle-repair interests; these are not a blanket repeal of all testing safeguards.','A fitness certificate assesses prescribed roadworthiness, not merely the vehicle’s age or registration validity.','Digital records can deter sham tests only if footage, identities and actual measurement are verified.|For AP, accessible accredited capacity and transparent retesting/grievances matter for both safety and livelihoods.','Do not infer every older vehicle is immediately scrapped or a video upload guarantees accurate testing.',day=8,mains='How can vehicle-fitness reform improve safety without becoming a compliance-only exercise?')
add('Awards & Sports','01 · Anti-doping — consultation','Anti-doping draft: criminal offences versus sports sanctions','May consultation proposes amendments to the 2022 Act.','Draft offences address trafficking/administration of prohibited substances or methods linked to sport.|Aggravated proposed penalties address minors, commercial conduct or organised crime.|Separate proposed advertising/promotion offences complement the regulatory anti-doping system; comments were invited until 18 June.','A sports-rule violation, disqualification and a criminal conviction follow different legal elements and procedures.','Protect athletes from exploitative supply networks while preserving testing reliability, fair hearings and proportionality.|For AP sports administration, combine education and support for minors with qualified medical advice and safe reporting.','These are draft criminal provisions, not a claim that every positive test automatically attracts the proposed prison term.','How should anti-doping policy protect clean sport and athletes’ procedural rights?',day=19)
add('Polity & Governance','04 · Limited government','Constitutionalism is more than numerical majority','The issue revisits judicial observations about constitutional limits.','Constitutionalism requires public power to be justified and limited by constitutional rules.|Articles 13, 32 and 226 underpin judicial review; Article 14 protects equality and non-arbitrariness.|Majority support does not authorise the removal of fundamental rights or the basic structure through an otherwise impermissible act.','A written Constitution can exist without effective limited government; constitutional practice requires institutions and enforceable safeguards.','For Mains, reconcile representative legitimacy with minority protection and reasoned review rather than present courts and elections as mutually exclusive.|AP applications include fair consultation and non-arbitrary administration, not an assumption that a national observation invalidated a specific state policy.','Do not attribute a final holding to an oral remark or equate every restriction on executive power with judicial supremacy over all policy choices.','How can constitutional democracy respect electoral mandates without permitting unchecked power?',src='',slug='constitutionalism')
add('Polity & Governance','05 · Panchayat performance evidence','PAI 2.0: reference year, categories and local validation','The issue discusses the FY2023–24 Panchayat Advancement Index.','The index localises SDGs through nine thematic areas; editorial month is not performance reference year.|Categories run from Beginner below40 to Achiever at90or above, with intermediate Aspirant, Performer and Front Runner bands.|The article describes Gram Sabha validation and stronger portal-based cross-checks as improvements.','A composite score depends on weights, data validity and participation; ranking is not a causal estimate of a panchayat’s impact.','For AP, use village-level gaps to inform Gram Panchayat Development Plans and budget priorities.|Reward improvement without encouraging selective reporting or treating non-submission as proof of identical deprivation.','Do not confuse FY2023–24 findings with measured May2026 performance or present a magazine ranking as an independent village audit.','How can local performance indices improve decentralised planning rather than only ranking?',src='',slug='panchayat-advancement-index-pai-2')
add('Polity & Governance','06 · Election Commission independence','ECI appointments: committee design and pending review','The magazine covers observations during challenge to the2023appointment law.','Under the reviewed law, the selection committee comprises the Prime Minister, a Union Cabinet Minister and the Lok Sabha opposition leader as provided by the statute.|The earlier Anoop Baranwal arrangement involving the CJI was an interim judicial framework pending legislation.|Article324(2) makes presidential appointment subject to parliamentary law; constitutional removal safeguards differ between the CEC and other ECs.','Appointment independence, operational autonomy and removal security are related but distinct institutional safeguards.','Assess whether selection encourages credible independence through transparency, eligibility and reasoned institutional checks.|A hearing observation does not itself strike down the Act or restore an earlier committee composition.','Do not claim the CJI remains a member under the2023Act or state that a rhetorical courtroom observation is the final operative order.','Evaluate appointment design as a safeguard for election administration.',src='',slug='appointment-procedure-of-the-election-commission-of-india-eci')
add('Awards & Sports','02 · Sports governance institutions','Sports Board and Tribunal: institutions need operational capacity','The issue describes2026rules under the2025sports-governance Act.','The reviewed Board has a chairperson and two members, with a three-year term subject to its age/reappointment provisions.|Tribunal leadership and members have distinct age limits and five-year terms under the described framework.|Digital filing and enforceable orders are institutional tools; they do not alone settle every federation dispute.','Institutional independence requires fair appointment, athlete representation, clear jurisdiction and remedies, not only a new body’s name.','For AP sports bodies, accountability should encompass selection transparency, safe-sport protections and accessible appeals.|Separate the national statutory framework from a claim that every state association dispute is automatically transferred to the Tribunal.','Do not confuse the Board’s term/age rules with the Tribunal’s or treat draft anti-doping criminal penalties as these notified governance rules.','What makes sports governance genuinely athlete-centred?',src='',slug='sports-governance-in-india')
add('International Relations','01 · India–US strategic supply chains','India–US cooperation: critical minerals and strategic autonomy','The issue reviews relations alongside May’s critical-minerals framework.','The framework reported by PRS covers mining, processing, recycling, investment and scrap management.|Cooperation also spans defence technology, trusted connectivity and civil space, but individual programmes have different legal stages.|Tariffs, market access and mobility can remain contested even when security interests converge.','A strategic partnership does not make interests identical or give a framework the effect of a completed mine, refinery or free-trade treaty.','Assess value-chain bottlenecks such as separation/refining and technology alongside access to ore.|For AP, examine port/logistics and processing possibilities only after environmental, commercial and supply-chain appraisal.','Do not infer guaranteed mineral deliveries or an operative full bilateral FTA from a cooperation framework.','How can India diversify strategic supply chains while retaining policy autonomy?',src='PRS',slug='india-us-relations')
add('International Relations','02 · Nordic geography and cooperation','India–Nordic ties: five countries, different treaty channels','The third India–Nordic Summit was held in Oslo in May.','The Nordic participants are Denmark, Finland, Iceland, Norway and Sweden.|Cooperation covers green technology, innovation, ocean sectors and people-to-people links.|Norway/Iceland are EFTA members; Denmark/Finland/Sweden are EU members, so TEPA and EU channels are not interchangeable.','The Nordic region is not synonymous with Scandinavia, the EU or the entire membership of EFTA.','India can connect industrial transition with research, skills and investment, but summit ambitions require subsequent implementation.|For AP, offshore/marine and clean-industry applications should be presented as analytical opportunities, not automatically funded state projects.','Do not put all five Nordic countries into the EU or assume a summit partnership itself eliminates tariffs.','How can India translate Nordic cooperation into measurable green-industrial capability?',src='PRS',slug='india-nordic-relations')
add('International Relations','03 · UAE energy and maritime links','UAE partnership: reserves, trade systems and implementation','The15May visit produced energy, defence and maritime outcomes.','The reviewed outcomes include defence-industrial cooperation and strategic oil-storage arrangements/exploration.|A virtual trade corridor using MAITRI concerns interoperable trade/regulatory processes, not a new physical canal.|An announced US$5billion investment commitment must be distinguished from realised inflows and audited project completion.','Strategic reserves, commercial inventories and domestic production each address different energy-security risks.','AP’s Visakhapatnam petroleum infrastructure makes storage logistics a useful linkage, but specific expansion must be checked against the actual instrument.|Cooperation can diversify resilience while leaving India exposed to chokepoints, price volatility and concentration risk.','Do not count proposed foreign storage as already-filled domestic reserve capacity or treat a digital corridor as a defence alliance.','Evaluate India–UAE cooperation through energy resilience and diversified economic links.',src='PRS',slug='india-uae-relations-2')
add('International Relations','04 · Nuclear governance','NPT review deadlock does not terminate the treaty','The issue reports a consensus failure at the eleventh review conference.','The NPT combines non-proliferation, disarmament and peaceful-use pillars.|India is not an NPT party; IAEA safeguards apply under specific agreements and facility arrangements, not simply to every installation.|The CTBT’s opening for signature and its entry into force are distinct; India has not signed it.','A review conference evaluates treaty implementation; failure to adopt a final document is not legal withdrawal by every party.','Disarmament credibility and security concerns must be assessed together, without treating non-membership in one treaty as absence of all obligations.|Avoid copying undated membership totals or an overbroad assertion that an Additional Protocol places every Indian civilian facility under identical safeguards.','Do not say the NPT ceased operating because consensus failed or that India is an NPT-recognised nuclear-weapon state.','Why does nuclear governance require both credible non-proliferation and disarmament commitments?',src='',slug='nuclear-governance')
add('International Relations','05 · Oil coordination — announcement versus effect','UAE OPEC exit:28April announcement,1May effect','The official WAM extract distinguishes the two dates.','The UAE announced exit from OPEC and OPEC+ on28April, effective1May2026.|OPEC is a producer organisation; OPEC+ includes coordinated cooperation with non-OPEC producers.|The official statement describes gradual output adjustment guided by demand, not an instant unlimited supply surge.','Quota coordination, production capacity, actual output and benchmark prices are separate variables.','For India, more production flexibility could improve options, but shipping disruption, refining suitability and global demand can dominate near-term prices.|Do not copy a fixed oil-supply share or member count without its date and definition.','Exit does not mean UAE stops exporting oil, and it does not guarantee an immediate price fall for Indian consumers.','How do producer-coordination changes affect an import-dependent economy’s energy security?',src='WAM',slug='uae-leaves-opec-and-opec')
add('Economy','09 · Digital infrastructure and governance','DPI@2047: interoperability needs accountable institutions','The issue describes NITI Aayog’s strategic DPI roadmap.','The proposed phases are DPI2.0for2025–35 and DPI3.0for2035–47.|The framework links sector transformation to foundational identity, payments and consent-based data exchange.|Interoperability is a technical/institutional design goal, not permission to share every personal record without limits.','DPI is a reusable enabling layer; services built on it can remain exclusionary or poorly governed if access and remedies fail.','For AP, district-led demand aggregation should pair assisted/offline access with purpose limitation and usable grievance redress.|Measure actual service completion, cost and exclusion rather than accounts created or API calls alone.','Do not describe a roadmap as a newly binding data-sharing law or assume all DPI applications are automatically non-excludable public goods.','What governance principles make digital infrastructure genuinely inclusive?',src='',slug='digital-public-infrastructure-dpi-1')
add('Economy','10 · UPI — decade of infrastructure','UPI’s decade: scale, interoperability and consumer protection','The May edition carries forward the2016–2026anniversary.','NPCI developed UPI as an interoperable instant-payment framework with participating banks and providers.|UPI LITE, offline variants and visitor-wallet facilities have distinct designs and eligibility conditions.|Transaction count, value and share of a specifically defined payments market must not be interchanged.','Authentication confirms a credential/action; it does not eliminate deception-based fraud or guarantee merchant credit access.','For AP’s small merchants, reliability and assisted dispute resolution matter as much as acceptance QR codes.|Zero-MDR policy raises funding and competition questions; large volume alone does not establish cost recovery or a provider’s solvency.','Do not claim every UPI transaction works offline, every payment is a bank deposit or digital logs guarantee a loan.','How can UPI sustain scale while protecting trust, competition and inclusion?',src='',slug='unified-payments-interface-upi-3')
add('Economy','11 · IIP — base, deflator and publication chronology','IIP rebasing: May coverage includes later June developments','Independent MoSPI text prevents treating a scheduled May release as an actual May launch.','The new series uses2022–23as base and expands industrial coverage; the later primary release confirms first launch on1June.|On29June, the PPI-based series superseded the initial WPI-deflated2022–23series;234of463item groups use value-based production, representing36.02%weight.|These are later chronology checks in a May-indexed magazine note, not claims the June facts were already known on28May.','An index’s base, weights, sector coverage, deflator and vintage jointly determine comparability; an index level is not its growth rate.','Do not mechanically splice old quarterly growth from the April release to the revised new-series level.|Deflating value-based production is necessary to distinguish price changes from real output; changes to the deflator can revise the history.','The original28May calendar date was a schedule, not independently verified launch; do not label the old manufacturing weight as the new series weight.','How do base-year and deflator revisions improve measurement while complicating comparisons?',src='PRS,IIPJUN',slug='all-india-index-of-industrial-production-iip')
add('Agriculture','04 · Rural investment and diversification','Rural resilience: finance, organisations and non-farm work','The issue discusses IFAD’s country-strategy framework and NABARD cooperation.','COSOP is a multi-year country strategic framework, not itself a direct universal household grant.|The described priorities include climate/social resilience and scaling proven development knowledge.|SHGs, FPOs and cooperatives can connect people with finance, markets and infrastructure, but have different memberships and functions.','Rural livelihoods combine agriculture, wage work, enterprise and care; output growth in one sector need not absorb all underemployed workers.','AP applications should integrate local water/climate risk, producer organisations and viable non-farm demand.|Avoid deriving near-zero productivity for every agricultural worker merely from mismatched aggregate GDP and employment shares.','A strategy or memorandum is not proof that all rural households obtained affordable investment credit.','How can rural investment support resilient income diversification?',src='',slug='strengthening-rural-economy')
add('Agriculture','05 · Producer organisations and markets','FPO viability: membership is not commercial sustainability','The issue reviews NAAS recommendations on producer organisations.','FPOs aggregate farmer demand/supply for inputs, marketing, processing and shared services.|Producer-company and cooperative legal forms are distinct; the magazine’s older PartIXAreference should not be used as the current2013Act chapter citation.|Professional management, working capital, member trust and quality control influence business viability.','A legal entity’s registration, number of members and turnover do not by themselves establish net member benefit or bargaining power.','AP FPOs can evaluate paddy/horticulture/fisheries opportunities only with market-specific logistics, standards and buyer risk controls.|Performance support should reward member transactions, transparent pricing and durable cash flows rather than registration targets alone.','Do not promise a fixed20–40%input saving or elimination of every intermediary for every FPO.','What makes producer collectivisation economically viable and democratically accountable?',src='',slug='farmers-producer-organisations-fpos')
add('Economy','12 · Bank capital and risk','Bank capital: CET1 is not cash or a reserve ratio','The issue covers revised treatment of quarterly profits in capital calculations.','CRAR compares eligible regulatory capital with risk-weighted assets; CET1is the highest-quality core component.|Risk-weighted assets are exposures adjusted for prescribed risks, not the capital a bank is required to hold.|Minimum baseline ratios and additional buffers/applicability conditions must be identified separately.','Capital absorbs losses; liquidity meets payment demands. A well-capitalised bank can still face a liquidity crisis.','Permission to recognise eligible interim profits can change measured capital but does not create new cash or remove provisioning requirements.|AP-linked credit analysis should examine asset quality, capital composition and lending standards, not assume a ratio relaxation forces lending.','Do not call CRAR the CRR/SLR, or infer high capital alone guarantees no bank can fail.','Why must bank resilience be assessed through both solvency and liquidity?',src='',slug='capital-adequacy-norms-for-banks')
add('Security','01 · Maritime strategy and public policy','INMSS2026: maritime security beyond a war–peace binary','The issue reviews the Navy’s new public maritime strategy.','It follows public strategic articulations in2007and2015.|The discussion covers grey-zone competition, cyber/disruptive technologies and sea-lane security.|Whole-of-government coordination includes naval, coastal, commercial and other agencies with distinct mandates.','Sea control, sea denial, deterrence, coastal policing and humanitarian assistance are different tasks requiring different capabilities.','AP’s ports, fisheries and coastal communities make information-sharing and incident response important civilian linkages.|A strategy outlines objectives and concepts; it does not prove every platform, command arrangement or coastal sensor is operational.','Do not equate a strategy publication with a new statute or assume the Navy alone performs all maritime law enforcement.','How should India integrate maritime economic security with defence preparedness?',src='',slug='indian-navy-maritime-security-strategy-inmss-2026')
add('Security','02 · Financial crime — stages and denominators','Money laundering: attachment is not final confiscation','The issue discusses the ED’s FY2025–26report.','A prosecution complaint, a completed trial and a conviction count are distinct stages.|Asset attachment and final confiscation/restoration have different legal consequences.|A conviction percentage needs its completed-case denominator; it is not automatically the share of all registered investigations ending in conviction.','Money laundering concerns proceeds of crime; a suspicious transaction alert is an investigative signal, not itself a judgment of guilt.','Compare enforcement statistics across matched periods and legal stages before judging deterrence or agency performance.|Preserve procedural safeguards, timely trials and independent oversight alongside better financial intelligence.','Do not call attached assets money already recovered by victims or extend a94%completed-trial ratio to the entire pending caseload.','How should financial-crime enforcement balance effectiveness, due process and credible performance reporting?',src='',slug='money-laundering')
add('Environment','02 · Circular material systems','Circular economy: reduce and repair before recycling','The issue discusses industry proposals for stronger coordination.','Circularity includes durability, sharing, reuse, repair, refurbishment and material recovery.|A proposed nodal ministry/agency is not an already-created institution.|EPR assigns specified producer obligations under applicable rules, not identical duties for every waste stream.','Recycling is one stage in a material hierarchy; avoiding waste and extending useful product life can reduce primary extraction earlier.','AP urban planning should connect segregation, viable recovery markets, worker safety and procurement standards.|Measure actual recovery, quality and displacement of virgin inputs rather than certificates or gross collected tonnage alone.','Do not equate waste-to-energy incineration with material recycling or repeat a projected2050market value as present output.','How can circular-economy policy align producer incentives with local waste-system capacity?',src='',slug='circular-economy')
