add('Society & Welfare','06 · Demography — reference and denominators','SRS2024: fertility and mortality are not census counts','The issue reviews ORGI’s2024reference-year statistical report.','The article reports national TFR1.9,IMR24and under-five mortality28; retain the report year and secondary-reading scope.|CBRand CDRuse population denominators; infant/under-five mortality use live-birth denominators.|Neonatal mortality concerns the first28completed days, not a loose birth-to29-days definition.','TFRis a synthetic period fertility measure; it is not current population growth, completed family size for every woman or a census headcount.','Sub-replacement fertility can coexist with population growth because age structure and momentum matter.|For AP, connect reproductive choice, maternal/child survival, migration and ageing rather than infer a cash incentive alone restores replacement fertility.','Do not assign the2024report estimates to observed May2026births or claim TFRbelow2.1means immediate population decline.','How should states respond to demographic transition while protecting health and reproductive autonomy?',src='',slug='sample-registration-system-srs-statistical-report-2024')
add('Society & Welfare','07 · Care infrastructure','Care economy: recognising work without inventing GDP','The issue reviews an EAC-PMworking paper on care.','Care includes paid services and unpaid household support for children, older people, disability and illness.|Time-use estimates must identify activity, participant/population denominator, sex and reference year.|Proposals for financing or parental-leave reform in a working paper are not automatically enacted entitlements.','Most unpaid household services fall outside conventional GDPproduction boundaries; satellite valuation is not an amount already included in measured GDP.','AP can assess childcare, eldercare, worker conditions and service quality as employment-enabling infrastructure.|Redistribution must include men, employers and public services, rather than merely rewarding women for remaining default carers.','Do not use the older PLFSself-employment-hours gap as unpaid-care time or turn an estimated15–17%valuation into official GDPgrowth.','Why should care be treated as social infrastructure rather than only a private household responsibility?',src='',slug='care-economy')
